# Aletheon Report: semaglutide

_Generated 2026-06-11 10:07 · 83 evidence chunks from 47 sources (section-targeted, detailed) · **degraded retrieval** (7/9 sources responded; unavailable: pharmgkb, semanticscholar) · grounded in retrieved sources only_

## Summary
Semaglutide is indicated for the treatment of adults with overweight or obesity, and type 2 diabetes. In a randomized controlled trial (n=304), semaglutide 2.4 mg once weekly resulted in a percentage change in body weight of -15.2% compared to -2.6% with placebo, yielding an estimated treatment difference of -12.6 percentage points (95% CI, -15.3 to -9.8; P < 0.0001) [E10]. The most important safety signal is gastrointestinal adverse events, reported in 82.2% of patients treated with semaglutide compared to 53.9% with placebo [E10]. Evidence strength is derived from 19 peer-reviewed studies and 63 regulatory sources.

