- In a trial of adults with obesity or overweight and at least one weight-related comorbidity (n=304), semaglutide 2.4 mg once weekly led to a weight loss of -15.2% compared to -2.6% for placebo, with a treatment difference of -12.6 percentage points (95% CI, -15.3 to -9.8; P < 0.0001) [E10] **(Confidence: High — supported by peer-reviewed RCT)**.
- Semaglutide significantly improved HbA1c levels in patients with type 2 diabetes, achieving HbA1c <7.0% in 67% of subjects compared to 40% for exenatide ER [E42] **(Confidence: High — supported by peer-reviewed RCT)**.
- In patients with type 2 diabetes at high cardiovascular risk, semaglutide reduced the occurrence of major adverse cardiovascular events (MACE) with a hazard ratio of 0.86 (95% CI: 0.77–0.96) [E34] **(Confidence: High — supported by regulatory evidence)**.
- Gastrointestinal adverse events were reported more frequently with semaglutide (82.2%) than with placebo (53.9%) [E10] **(Confidence: High — supported by peer-reviewed RCT)**.
- Discontinuation due to adverse events occurred in 15.5% of semaglutide-treated patients compared to 11.6% in the placebo group [E21] **(Confidence: High — supported by regulatory evidence)**.

