| E12 | DailyMed: ADVERSE REACTIONS | Adverse reactions reported during post-approval use include acute pancreatitis, anaphylaxis, and acute kidney injury. |
| E13 | DailyMed: Severe Gastrointestinal Adverse… | Severe gastrointestinal adverse reactions have also been reported postmarketing with GLP-1 receptor agonists. |
| E14 | FDA Label: OZEMPIC | Gastrointestinal adverse reactions occurred more frequently among patients who received semaglutide tablets than placebo. |
| E21 | DailyMed: Clinical Trials Experience | Safety data collection was limited to serious adverse events (including death), adverse events leading to discontinuation, and adverse events of special interest. |
| E22 | DailyMed: WARNINGS AND PRECAUTIONS | Serious hypersensitivity reactions (e.g., anaphylaxis, angioedema) have been reported in patients treated with semaglutide tablets. |
| E27 | Oral semaglutide versus subcutaneous liraglutide… | Safety was assessed in all participants who received at least one dose of study drug. |
| E29 | Effect of Subcutaneous Semaglutide vs Placebo as… | Gastrointestinal adverse events were more frequent with semaglutide (82.8%) vs placebo (63.2%). |
| E30 | Effect of Weekly Subcutaneous Semaglutide vs… | Proportions of participants discontinuing treatment for any reason were 13.5% with semaglutide and 27.6% with liraglutide. |
| E40 | Efficacy and safety of once-weekly semaglutide… | No deaths were reported in any of the study groups and most reported adverse events were of mild or moderate severity. |
| E42 | Efficacy and Safety of Once-Weekly Semaglutide… | Both treatments had similar safety profiles, but gastrointestinal adverse events were more common in semaglutide-treated subjects than in exenatide ER-treated subjects. |
| E44 | FDA Label: WEGOVY | WEGOVY lowers blood glucose and can cause hypoglycemia, with increased risk when used with insulin or insulin secretagogues. |
| E49 | DailyMed: DRUG INTERACTIONS | Patients receiving RYBELSUS or OZEMPIC tablets in combination with an insulin secretagogue or insulin may have an increased risk of hypoglycemia, including severe hypoglycemia. |

- Common adverse effects include nausea (20% with semaglutide 0.5 mg, 24% with 1.0 mg) and gastrointestinal issues such as diarrhea (13% with 0.5 mg, 11% with 1.0 mg) [E40].
- Severe gastrointestinal adverse reactions occurred in 2% of patients treated with semaglutide 14 mg compared to 0.3% in placebo [E22].
- Serious hypersensitivity reactions (e.g., anaphylaxis, angioedema) have been reported; semaglutide is contraindicated in patients with a prior serious hypersensitivity reaction to the drug [E22].
- Acute gallbladder disease, including cholelithiasis and cholecystitis, has been reported in clinical trials [E22].
- There is an increased risk of hypoglycemia when semaglutide is used with insulin or insulin secretagogues [E48].

## Black Box Warnings
No FDA boxed warning is present in this drug's prescribing information.

## Cardiovascular Risk Profile
- In patients with type 2 diabetes at high cardiovascular risk, semaglutide reduced the occurrence of MACE with a hazard ratio of 0.86 (95% CI: 0.77–0.96) [E34].
- Nonfatal myocardial infarction occurred in 2.9% of patients receiving semaglutide versus 3.9% in the placebo group (hazard ratio, 0.74; 95% CI, 0.51 to 1.08; P=0.12) [E24].
- Nonfatal stroke rates were 1.6% in the semaglutide group compared to 2.7% in the placebo group (hazard ratio, 0.61; 95% CI, 0.38 to 0.99; P=0.04) [E24].

## Pharmacokinetics & Pharmacodynamics
- Semaglutide has an absolute bioavailability of 89% following subcutaneous administration [E80].
- The elimination half-life of semaglutide is approximately one week, with a mean clearance of about 0.05 L/h [E80].
- Semaglutide is extensively bound to plasma albumin (>99%), which results in decreased renal clearance and protection from degradation [E80].
- Maximum concentration of semaglutide is reached 1 to 3 days post-dose [E80].

## Dosing & Administration
- Semaglutide is administered as a subcutaneous injection at a dose of 2.4 mg once weekly for weight management in adults with obesity or overweight [E10].
- For type 2 diabetes, semaglutide is available in doses of 0.5 mg or 1.0 mg once weekly [E40].

## Drug Interactions
- Semaglutide delays gastric emptying, which may impact the absorption of concomitantly administered oral medications [E48].
- Levothyroxine exposure was increased by 33% (90% CI: 1.25–1.42) when administered with semaglutide [E48].
- Caution is advised when semaglutide is used with insulin or insulin secretagogues due to an increased risk of hypoglycemia [E48].

## Mechanism of Action
Semaglutide is a GLP-1 analogue with 94% sequence homology to human GLP-1. It acts as a GLP-1 receptor agonist, stimulating insulin secretion and reducing glucagon secretion in a glucose-dependent manner. This mechanism helps lower blood glucose levels and promotes weight loss by regulating appetite and caloric intake [E2][E3].

## Pregnancy, Lactation & Reproductive Safety
- Semaglutide should be used during pregnancy only if the potential benefit justifies the potential risk to the fetus. Animal studies indicate potential risks, including embryofetal mortality and structural abnormalities [E56].
- Breastfeeding is not recommended during treatment with semaglutide due to the unknown potential for serious adverse reactions in the breastfed infant [E70].

## Use in Specific Populations
- No overall differences in safety or effectiveness have been observed between patients aged 65 years and older and younger adult patients [E55].
- The recommended dosage of semaglutide is the same for patients with renal or hepatic impairment as for those with normal function [E55].

## Contradictions & Disagreements
No direct contradictions found in current sources.

## Preprint / Emerging Evidence (not yet peer-reviewed)
No preprint evidence in current sources.

## Clinical Bottom Line
Use semaglutide for weight management in adults with obesity or overweight at a dose of 2.4 mg once weekly. The primary risk to monitor is gastrointestinal adverse events, reported in 82.2% of patients. Avoid or use cautiously in patients with a history of severe gastrointestinal disorders or hypersensitivity reactions.

